ic":"science","genre":"news_report","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C1-004","subskill":"inference","difficulty":4,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Why have so many papyri survived at Oxyrhynchus?","options":["Rainless conditions kept the discarded material safe under sand.","They were stored in the town library, which was a centre of learning.","Papyrus made from Nile reeds lasts longer than most writing materials.","Volcanic ash sealed them, just as it did at Herculaneum."],"answer":"Rainless conditions kept the discarded material safe under sand.","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"R-C1-004-Q4","level":"C1","skill":"reading","category":"Reading","topic":"science","genre":"news_report","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C1-004","subskill":"implicit_meaning","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"What does the phrase ''the random waste-paper of seven centuries'' suggest about the collection?","options":["It consists mainly of literary works that were thrown away by mistake.","It is too badly damaged to tell historians much about daily life.","Most of it is everyday documents rather than literature.","Scholars gathered the documents over a period of seven centuries."],"answer":"Most of it is everyday documents rather than literature.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"R-C2-003-Q1","level":"C2","skill":"reading","category":"Reading","topic":"culture","genre":"critical_essay","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C2-003","subskill":"nuanced_stance","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Which statement best captures the writer’s position?","options":["Later versions are usually more authentic because they reflect experience.","First versions deserve special weight because they are the closest to the maker''s intention.","Versions offer different evidence, so being earliest does not settle authenticity.","Authenticity is best judged by the version the creator finally chose."],"answer":"Versions offer different evidence, so being earliest does not settle authenticity.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"R-C2-003-Q2","level":"C2","skill":"reading","category":"Reading","topic":"culture","genre":"critical_essay","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C2-003","subskill":"subtle_implication","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"What is implied by the phrase ''an archive’s accidents''?","options":["Digital archives keep so many versions that the first is hard to identify.","Archivists sometimes damage early drafts while trying to preserve them.","Creators conceal failed attempts so that later work looks spontaneous.","What happens to survive can make a later stage look like the beginning."],"answer":"What happens to survive can make a later stage look like the beginning.","answerKey":"D","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":2,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"R-C2-003-Q3","level":"C2","skill":"reading","category":"Reading","topic":"culture","genre":"critical_essay","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C2-003","subskill":"rhetorical_structure","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Why does the writer discuss both beneficial and harmful revision?","options":["to show that revision must be judged on its effects, not on chronology","to argue that artists revise for better reasons than institutions do","to show that revision tends to remove the energy of an early version","to suggest preferring later versions when experience exposes ambiguities"],"answer":"to show that revision must be judged on its effects, not on chronology","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"R-C2-003-Q4","level":"C2","skill":"reading","category":"Reading","topic":"culture","genre":"critical_essay","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C2-003","subskill":"conceptual_inference","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"What does the writer mean by saying that “intention itself moved”?","options":["Creators often lose sight of what they originally aimed for during long revisions.","A work''s purpose can develop through making and revision, not exist from the start.","Version histories reveal the single intention behind each change.","Intentions can no longer be studied once several versions exist."],"answer":"A work''s purpose can develop through making and revision, not exist from the start.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"R-C2-004-Q1","level":"C2","skill":"reading","category":"Reading","topic":"society","genre":"philosophical_essay","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C2-004","subskill":"main_argument","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Which statement best captures Mill''s central claim in this passage?","options":["A majority may silence an opinion once almost everyone agrees it is false.","Governments may suppress opinions that the public largely rejects.","Silencing any opinion wrongs everyone, since it assumes a certainty no one has.","Since opinions belong to individuals, silencing one is merely a private injury."],"answer":"Silencing any opinion wrongs everyone, since it assumes a certainty no one has.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"R-C2-004-Q2","level":"C2","skill":"reading","category":"Reading","topic":"society","genre":"philosophical_essay","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C2-004","subskill":"rhetorical_purpose","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Why does Mill mention a man who would be ''a Churchman in London'' but ''a Buddhist or a Confucian in Pekin''?","options":["to show that people''s certainties often reflect where they happen to live","to show that religious belief is stronger in some countries than in others","to argue that people should travel in order to understand other beliefs","to show that individuals trust their own judgment more than their society''s"],"answer":"to show that people''s certainties often reflect where they happen to live","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":2,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"R-C2-004-Q3","level":"C2","skill":"reading","category":"Reading","topic":"society","genre":"philosophical_essay","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C2-004","subskill":"implicit_meaning","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"According to Mill, what is lost when a false opinion is silenced?","options":["the opportunity of exchanging an error for the truth itself","the chance for the minority to persuade the generations that come after them","little of value, since the error is simply removed from debate","a clearer, livelier grasp of the truth that collision with error gives"],"answer":"a clearer, livelier grasp of the truth that collision with error gives","answerKey":"D","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"R-C2-004-Q4","level":"C2","skill":"reading","category":"Reading","topic":"society","genre":"philosophical_essay","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"R-C2-004","subskill":"nuanced_inference","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"What does Mill imply about people who ''sometimes hear their opinions disputed''?","options":["They no longer assume that their own views are infallible.","They still treat the views shared by their own circle as beyond doubt.","They trust their solitary judgment more than absolute princes do.","They rely more on their own judgment than on the views of the people around them."],"answer":"They still treat the views shared by their own circle as beyond doubt.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-A1-001-Q1","level":"A1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"daily_life","genre":"dialogue","audience":"general","taskletExternalId":"L-A1-001","subskill":"specific_information","difficulty":2,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"How many cheese rolls do