"skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"general","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-002","subskill":"main_argument","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What is Osei''s main criticism of designing for an ''average user''?","options":["Designers often use the word ''average'' to avoid conducting more detailed research with users.","An average may describe a population without corresponding to any real person''s characteristics.","Products designed from averages tend to cost more because they require unnecessary adjustments.","Average measurements are usually unreliable because designers collect data from samples that are too small."],"answer":"An average may describe a population without corresponding to any real person''s characteristics.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-002-Q2","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"general","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-002","subskill":"inference","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What does Osei mean by saying good design often needs ''ranges''?","options":["The most successful products are those that can serve several unrelated purposes equally well.","Every product should be produced in enough versions to fit every potential user exactly.","Design should accommodate meaningful variation rather than optimise only for a central value.","Designers should replace numerical measurements with direct observation of individual users."],"answer":"Design should accommodate meaningful variation rather than optimise only for a central value.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":2,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-002-Q3","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"general","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-002","subskill":"nuanced_stance","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"How does Osei view the goal of inclusive design?","options":["It requires every product to work equally well for every conceivable user.","It is mainly a marketing strategy for offering several versions of one product.","It should reduce unnecessary exclusion without claiming all limits can disappear.","It should prioritise users at the statistical extremes over everyone else."],"answer":"It should reduce unnecessary exclusion without claiming all limits can disappear.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-002-Q4","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"general","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-002","subskill":"implicit_meaning","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What changes when exclusion becomes ''a decision you can examine''?","options":["Users become legally responsible for choosing products that suit their needs.","Designers can justify or revise a limitation instead of treating it as a default.","A product can be described as inclusive even when no changes are made.","Statistical averages become unnecessary because all design decisions are subjective."],"answer":"Designers can justify or revise a limitation instead of treating it as a default.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-001-Q1","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-001","subskill":"nuanced_stance","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What distinction does Moretti draw between useful complexity and unhelpful complexity?","options":["Extra factors matter when they change how causes are weighted, not when they merely add detail.","Any factor supported by evidence should be included regardless of its effect on the conclusion.","Complex explanations are useful only when they contain fewer than a certain number of causes.","A single cause is acceptable in public communication but never in historical scholarship."],"answer":"Extra factors matter when they change how causes are weighted, not when they merely add detail.","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-001-Q2","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-001","subskill":"subtle_implication","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What does Moretti mean by saying clean explanations are ''portable''?","options":["They can be separated from moral judgement more easily than complex explanations can.","They are easy to repeat, remember and reuse in simplified public accounts.","They can be applied accurately to many different historical cases.","They remain valid when translated into several languages and cultural contexts."],"answer":"They are easy to repeat, remember and reuse in simplified public accounts.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":2,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-001-Q3","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-001","subskill":"rhetorical_function","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why does Moretti reject simply listing forty causes?","options":["Because long lists encourage people to search for heroes and villains.","Because explaining means judging importance, not passing that task to the audience.","Because most events have only a small number of causes that can be empirically verified.","Because audiences are incapable of remembering more than a few historical facts."],"answer":"Because explaining means judging importance, not passing that task to the audience.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-001-Q4","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-001","subskill":"implicit_meaning","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why can contrary evidence feel like an attempt to ''weaken the lesson''?","options":["A moralised explanation can make nuance feel like a threat to the lesson people value.","Moral lessons become unreliable whenever an explanation involves more than one significant cause.","Scholars often introduce new evidence specifically to weaken the political force of public stories.","Historical evidence becomes less credible when it conflicts with a widely shared ethical judgement."],"answer":"A moralised explanation can make nuance feel like a threat to the lesson people value.","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-002-Q1","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-002","subskill":"conceptual_understanding","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What is Bell''s main point about silence?","options":["It is usually a sign that participants are afraid to disagree with authority.","It has become more difficult to interpret because most communication now occurs online.","Its meaning depends on interaction and context, not just an absence of words.","Its duration provides the most reliable evidence of what a speaker intends."],"answer":"Its meaning depends on interaction and context, not just an absence of words.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-002-Q2","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-002","subskill":"subtle_inference","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why does Bell compare a pause after an invitation with a pause after a factual question?","options":["to suggest that reluctance can only be expressed indirectly through silence","to argue that factual questions should be answered immediately in professional settings","to demonstrate that invitations produce longer pauses than questions in every language","to show that 