ion at the end?","options":["that a daily fine necessarily makes people return books","that libraries should collect data about policy changes","that public services need rules that are easy to understand","that reminders can help borrowers remember due dates"],"answer":"that a daily fine necessarily makes people return books","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-003-Q1","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":null,"audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-003","subskill":"main_argument","difficulty":4,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What is the researcher primarily interested in?","options":["whether predictive text shapes wording while ideas are still being expressed","whether predictive systems contain more spelling errors than dictionaries","whether people can learn to type without any automated assistance","whether email templates are replacing other forms of workplace communication"],"answer":"whether predictive text shapes wording while ideas are still being expressed","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-003-Q2","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":null,"audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-003","subskill":"nuanced_inference","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why does the researcher mention suggestions that users reject?","options":["to show that rejected suggestions are stored and used to retrain the system","to suggest that just seeing an option may affect which alternatives feel available","to argue that prediction fails because most users ignore its recommendations","to show that writers remember rejected phrases better than accepted ones"],"answer":"to suggest that just seeing an option may affect which alternatives feel available","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":2,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-003-Q3","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":null,"audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-003","subskill":"speaker_attitude","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"How does the researcher view comparisons with dictionaries?","options":["They prove predictive text is simply another harmless writing tool.","They are irrelevant because dictionaries no longer influence writing.","They show that all writing technologies should be evaluated in the same way.","They are useful but miss how continuous and proactive prediction is."],"answer":"They are useful but miss how continuous and proactive prediction is.","answerKey":"D","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-003-Q4","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":null,"audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-003","subskill":"implication","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What does the researcher mean by saying “ease is doing part of the deciding”?","options":["Writers become unable to make decisions once prediction is enabled.","Predictive systems deliberately choose ideas on behalf of users.","Convenient suggestions can shape choices without determining them.","Efficient writing always reduces the originality of the final text."],"answer":"Convenient suggestions can shape choices without determining them.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-003-Q1","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"science","genre":null,"audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-003","subskill":"nuanced_stance","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What distinction is central to Morris’s argument?","options":["between disagreement caused by ignorance and disagreement caused by dishonesty","between scientific disagreement and all forms of political disagreement","between experts who communicate confidently and experts who avoid public debate","between disagreement that exposes assumptions and disagreement signalling failure"],"answer":"between disagreement that exposes assumptions and disagreement signalling failure","answerKey":"D","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.5","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-003-Q2","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"science","genre":null,"audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-003","subskill":"subtle_implication","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why does Morris contrast the novice’s single number with the expert’s range?","options":["to imply that precise numerical claims are never scientifically useful","to argue that novices communicate more effectively with the public","to show that recognising conditions and uncertainty can reflect deeper knowledge","to suggest that experts deliberately make answers complica